From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: mtk_wdt: stop a watchdog left running by the bootloader on suspend

The bootloader can leave the watchdog running, which the driver notes as
WDOG_HW_RUNNING at probe. Nobody has to open the device then: the watchdog
core pings it from a timer. Suspend and resume only look at
watchdog_active(), so the watchdog keeps counting while the system sleeps,
the timer does not fire, and the watchdog resets the system.

Seen on the Lenovo TB375FC (MT6897), where LK leaves WDT_MODE at 0x7d. It
restarted about a minute after entering s2idle.

Treat a running watchdog like an active one, as da9062 and da9063 do.
---
 drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
--- a/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
@@ -473,7 +473,8 @@ static int mtk_wdt_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct mtk_wdt_dev *mtk_wdt = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-	if (watchdog_active(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev))
+	if (watchdog_active(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev) ||
+	    watchdog_hw_running(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev))
 		mtk_wdt_stop(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -483,7 +484,8 @@ static int mtk_wdt_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct mtk_wdt_dev *mtk_wdt = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-	if (watchdog_active(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev)) {
+	if (watchdog_active(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev) ||
+	    watchdog_hw_running(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev)) {
 		mtk_wdt_start(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev);
 		mtk_wdt_ping(&mtk_wdt->wdt_dev);
 	}
